# Static redirects, applied by Cloudflare's asset server. It consumes this file
# rather than serving it, so it never appears at /_redirects — and it runs
# BEFORE worker.js, which is why a rule here can shadow a Worker route (and
# why none of them do).
#
# /desktops was a page until 2026-08-12, then the landing page's first
# section; since 2026-08-14 that section is one sentence and one link, and
# the comparison lives at /docs/haus/desktops/choosing. ⚠️ The target moved on
# 2026-08-17 and the section moved with it: the site split into the house (`/`)
# and the layer (`/haus`), and Desktops is a fact about the layer. The id is
# unchanged, so this is the same anchor one floor down — and it stays that way
# whatever #desktops becomes, because this URL has been shared since
# 2026-08-08. Both spellings are listed because the
# no-slash form only 307s to the slash form while an index.html exists at
# that path, and it no longer does.
#
# 🚨 Exact paths only, never `/desktops/*`. The three deep pages below need
# three DIFFERENT targets, so a wildcard could not serve them even now that
# they are all redirects — and the day one of them comes back as a page, a
# wildcard would 301 it away from itself.
/desktops     /haus/#desktops   301
/desktops/    /haus/#desktops   301

# The three desktop pages, deleted 2026-08-14 — hours after `everyday` and
# `minimal` landed, and the same day the catalogue in front of them was cut.
# The user's call, and the reasoning is the one that retired /haus and
# /pounce before them: the docs and the landing page are the only surfaces
# this site keeps, and a desktop is a set of values for haus's own options,
# which is a subject the haus tree already carries a section on. Each page's
# copy went to its own page under /docs/haus/desktops/ rather than into one
# shared table, so each URL has an exact destination.
#
# These were published, canonical and shared from 2026-08-14. They keep
# resolving forever.
#
# ⚠️ NOT affected, and must not be: /hacker.sh, /everyday.sh and /minimal.sh,
# which are worker.js routes and are what a `curl | bash` in someone's shell
# history actually calls. Deleting a page never touches an installer URL.
/desktops/hacker      /docs/haus/desktops/hacker/      301
/desktops/hacker/     /docs/haus/desktops/hacker/      301
/desktops/everyday    /docs/haus/desktops/everyday/    301
/desktops/everyday/   /docs/haus/desktops/everyday/    301
/desktops/minimal     /docs/haus/desktops/minimal/     301
/desktops/minimal/    /docs/haus/desktops/minimal/     301

# 🚨 /haus and /haus/ were 301s to /docs/haus/ from 2026-08-14 to 2026-08-17,
# and those two lines are DELETED rather than retargeted. The URL is a real
# page again: the site split into the house and the layer, and everything the
# landing page said about haus moved to `/haus`. Cloudflare reads this file
# ahead of the assets, so leaving the redirect in place would have 301'd the
# new page away from itself — a route existing does not beat a rule pointing
# elsewhere. If /haus is ever retired a second time, the lines come back.
#
# /docs itself is the redirect now, added 2026-08-17. It was a doorway page
# listing the four trees, and it went the same way `/` did: an index of the
# same four things, one click from a front page that already indexes them. The
# trees are unaffected — this is an exact path, so /docs/haus and everything
# under it is untouched — and the target is the layer's tree, which is the one
# a reader who typed /docs is most often after.
/docs         /docs/haus/  301
/docs/        /docs/haus/  301

